q .08 .14 .26 rg 0 792 595 50 re f Q
BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 01 | 40Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 60 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (36. During exercise, pulmonary blood flow increases greatly but pulmonary arterial pressure rises relatively) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (little. What helps explain this?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: B. Previously underperfused capillaries open and existing vessels widen.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Recruitment of additional pulmonary capillaries and distension of vessels increase the total flow) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (capacity, limiting the rise in pulmonary vascular resistance and pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Other options: Recruitment and distension expand, not reduce, pulmonary vascular capacity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 654.5 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0053 | Regional Circulation, pp. 30-32 \(concept extension\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 631.3 Tm (37. After firm stroking of the skin, redness spreads beyond the directly stimulated line. What produces this) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 617.6 Tm (surrounding flare?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 600.9 Tm (Answer: A. An axon reflex causing nearby arterioles to dilate) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 584.6 Tm (Explanation: The flare of the triple response is produced by an antidromic axon reflex that dilates vessels around) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 571.5 Tm (the stimulated region.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 555.4 Tm (Other options: The wheal reflects increased local permeability, whereas the flare is spreading arteriolar vasodilation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 540.8 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0055 | Regional Circulation, pp. 39-40) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 517.6 Tm (38. What change occurs in the pulmonary circulation with the newborn's first breaths?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 500.9 Tm (Answer: C. Pulmonary vascular resistance falls as the lungs expand and oxygen rises.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 484.6 Tm (Explanation: Lung expansion and increased alveolar oxygen reduce pulmonary vascular resistance, raising pulmonary) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 471.5 Tm (blood flow. Loss of the placenta simultaneously tends to raise systemic vascular resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 455.4 Tm (Other options: The first breaths promote pulmonary vasodilation, not vasoconstriction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 440.8 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0057 | Regional Circulation, pp. 42-45 \(concept extension\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.6 Tm (39. Which pair of electrodes forms standard bipolar lead I in an ECG?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 400.9 Tm (Answer: D. Right arm \(negative\) and left arm \(positive\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 384.6 Tm (Explanation: Standard lead I measures the potential difference LA\(+\) minus RA\(-\). Lead II uses RA\(-\) and LL\(+\),) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 371.5 Tm (while lead III uses LA\(-\) and LL\(+\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.6 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 355.4 Tm (Other options: Lead II and III use the left-leg electrode; reversing the lead I polarity is also incorrect.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (Source: DOM-CVS-0058 | ECG 2, pp. 3-5) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 317.6 Tm (40. In the ECG strip, the distance between consecutive R waves is 20 small squares. The paper speed is 25) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 303.9 Tm (mm/s. What is the heart rate?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 287.2 Tm (Answer: B. 75 beats/min) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 270.9 Tm (Explanation: At 25 mm/s, one small square = 0.04 s. Twenty squares = 0.8 s per beat, giving 60/0.8 = 75 beats/min.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 257.8 Tm (Equivalently, 1500/20 = 75 beats/min.) Tj ET
